Theorem 1.66. For any nonempty A, the following are equivalent:

   1. A is countable;
   2. there exists a surjection f : N → A;
   3. there exists an injection g : A → N.

Definition 2.25. Dendrogram (finite case). Let X be finite. A dendrogram
on X is an order preserving map θ : [0, ∞) → P art(X) on X such that:
  1. θ(0) is the discrete partition of X;
  2. there exists T > 0 such that θ(T ) is the trivial partition;

  3. for any t ≥ 0, there exists ϵ > 0 such that θ(t) = θ(t + ϵ).

